      "card_ids": ["C34"],
      "claim": "Acarbose has negligible absolute oral bioavailability (<2%) in humans, but this reflects a highly polar, non-absorbable oligosaccharide acting locally in the gut; the query is a lipophilic macrolide with a macrocyclic lactone and methylated sugars, so this low-F mechanism does not transfer.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C18"],
      "claim": "Aminoglycosides such as tobramycin have poor oral bioavailability because of polycationic, highly polar sugar structures; the query's desosamine sugar is a dimethylamino sugar within a lipophilic macrolide, a class known to be orally absorbed, so this evidence does not establish low F for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "Glycyrrhizin has extremely low oral bioavailability (~0.25%) due to poor permeability and gut hydrolysis of the saponin glycoside; the query is an acid-stable macrolide oxime ether, not a hydrolyzable saponin, so the low-F result is not applicable.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C24"],
      "claim": "Fluticasone propionate has <1% oral bioavailability due to extensive first-pass metabolism; the query lacks the corticosteroid ester and is a macrolide antibiotic with high oral absorption in its class, so this low-F evidence is not transferable.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is roxithromycin, a macrolide antibiotic with an acid-stable oxime ether and two lipophilic sugars; macrolides of this class are well absorbed orally, with roxithromycin typically showing F well above 20%. The retrieved low-F records come from chemically distinct classes (aminoglycosides, glycosides, glycopeptides, corticosteroids, nucleotides) whose poor oral bioavailability arises from mechanisms not shared by the query. Therefore the prediction is high (F >= 20%).",
